commit 4aec72896d24967eadfe3091e2f563ef035d3474
Author: JBailes <jbailes@gmail.com>
Date:   Mon Aug 3 15:06:07 2026 +0200

    fix: close-on-exec accepted sockets so a forked child cannot hang the client (#2297)
    
    An accepted connection fd was inheritable. The CLI reads its response until
    EOF (http_uds_client.c ignores Content-Length), so EOF arrives only once every
    copy of the socket is closed — and aimee-server forks constantly (git,
    delegates, deploys, process_mgr). Any child forked while a request is in
    flight inherited that request's fd and pinned it open for the child's whole
    lifetime.
    
    Observed in production as `aimee workspace add` never returning: the server
    had already answered and closed its copy, but a background `git cat-file
    --batch` forked during the request still held the fd, and the client sat in
    read() for 28 minutes. Three benchmark lanes stalled on this, each on its
    first aimee cell. Short requests hide it — the window has to overlap a fork.
    
    accept4(SOCK_CLOEXEC) sets the flag atomically, which matters in a
    multi-threaded forking server: a post-accept fcntl() leaves a window in which
    another thread can fork and inherit anyway. macOS/BSD have no accept4, so
    fall back to accept()+fcntl() there.
    
    The helper lives in server_conn_io.c, which already owns per-connection fd
    lifecycle, so it is reachable from a test.
    
    aimee-kb's plaintext listener already did this; its mTLS listener did not —
    fixed here too. That listener carries aimee-server -> aimee-kb traffic in the
    managed topology, and aimee-kb forks a curator sidecar per symbol, so it is
    the most exposed listener in the system.
    
    The test asserts the observable failure, not the flag: it reproduces the hang
    on the stock accept() path and shows the fixed path releases the client.

